WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.432
Stored procedures can be useful database boundaries.

2
00:00:03.432 --> 00:00:14.389
They can centralize complex operations, reduce direct table access, support permission separation, and give database teams a clear place to review important behavior.

3
00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:20.264
But a stored procedure is not automatically safe simply because it lives in the database.

4
00:00:20.264 --> 00:00:24.686
Dynamic SQL inside stored procedures still needs safe construction.

5
00:00:24.686 --> 00:00:41.715
If a procedure accepts values that influence database command structure, the same defensive questions apply: which parts are approved structure, which parts are parameter values, and how does the procedure prevent callers from controlling more than intended.

6
00:00:41.715 --> 00:00:45.742
Parameterization remains important inside database workflows.

7
00:00:45.742 --> 00:00:49.306
Stored procedures also need careful permission design.

8
00:00:49.306 --> 00:00:54.256
Application accounts should not have broad database permissions by default.

9
00:00:54.256 --> 00:01:03.365
In some architectures, procedures can expose only the operations the application needs while limiting direct access to underlying objects.

10
00:01:03.365 --> 00:01:10.758
The right model depends on the application, but broad convenience permissions should be reviewed before release.

11
00:01:10.758 --> 00:01:18.810
Reporting, search, export, maintenance, and administrative procedures often combine flexible input with broad data access.

12
00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:26.467
They may run outside normal request flows, serve privileged users, or support operational needs under time pressure.

13
00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:33.662
These procedures deserve clear ownership, parameter review, logging expectations, and least-privilege design.

14
00:01:33.662 --> 00:01:37.820
Application and database teams share prevention responsibility.

15
00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:44.750
Application developers understand caller context, DTOs, validation, authorization, and business workflow.

16
00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:51.219
Database specialists understand permissions, procedure behavior, query plans, and data boundaries.

17
00:01:51.219 --> 00:01:57.423
Safe design improves when both sides review how application values become database operations.

18
00:01:57.423 --> 00:02:03.430
A good release process treats database objects as part of the application security surface.

19
00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:16.235
Procedure changes, permission changes, reporting changes, and migration scripts should be reviewed with the same seriousness as controller or service-layer code that constructs queries directly.
